  "body": "Third of four from the fallback audit. Two defects in the same subsystem, both of which made a configured chain unreachable.\n\n## 1. One shared budget, drained by the first model\n\n`FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT` was a single pool for the whole request, so a silent primary could spend all of it and every model behind it was skipped for lack of time — `MODEL CHAIN EXHAUSTED: request budget spent before trying …`.\n\nMeasured on 21 days of live traffic: **393 requests ran the full 600s budget having produced only scaffolding**, with a chain sitting unused.\n\nEach attempt now gets an equal share of what's left, counting itself and every model still behind it. Unused time flows forward, so a chain of fast failures leaves the last model nearly the whole budget rather than a third of it.\n\n```\nbefore   [ attempt 0 ................ 600s ] X   chain never reached\nafter    [ attempt 0 .. 200s ] X\n         [ attempt 1 .. 200s ] -> answers\n```\n\n**The share bounds time-to-first-content only.** Once a stream is producing, the attempt owns the rest of the budget — cutting a working answer to preserve a fallback that may not be working is the wrong trade. #166 is what makes \"producing\" mean *content* rather than any frame, which is why these two landed in this order.\n\n## 2. A bench that never happened\n\n`RouteHealthRegistry` ejects a model after N consecutive failures. The comment on its own factory warned that *\"a registry rebuilt per request would never reach its failure threshold and would eject nothing\"* — which is exactly what was happening, because `MessagesHandler` and everything it owns, including the executor and its registry, is constructed **per request** in `routes.py`.\n\nRegistries now live at module scope, keyed by the two settings that define ejection, so changing either starts a clean registry rather than inheriting benches made under a different policy. An autouse fixture clears them between tests, since this is process state.\n\n## Verification\n\nLive, same config, same four requests, reading the **loguru** log rather than uvicorn's:\n\n```\npre-fix  (fork/main)     MODEL FALLBACK: 12   MODEL EJECTED: 0\npost-fix (this branch)   MODEL FALLBACK: 12   MODEL EJECTED: 9\n```\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing **by name**:\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n| --- | --- |\n| M1 no share, one pool again | `silent_primary_cannot_spend_the_whole_budget` |\n| M2 share still applies after content | `first_token_deadline_stops_applying`, `chain_is_not_extended_once_spent` |\n| M3 registry per-request again | `benched_across_requests`, `policy_starts_clean` |\n| M4 one global registry regardless of policy | `benched_across_requests`, `policy_starts_clean` |\n\nOne existing test was **rewritten rather than deleted**: `test_the_chain_is_not_extended_once_the_budget_is_spent` pinned the old behaviour where the first model drains the pool — which is the defect. It now exercises the same invariant under the new rules.\n\nLocal CI: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, `ty` clean; pytest **4,815 passed** with the documented 6 `test_install_ps1_*` baseline failures.\n\n## Correction to #165\n\nThat PR reported *\"confirmed live: four consecutive failures produced zero `MODEL CHAIN: skipping` lines\"*. **That grep read the uvicorn log, which never contains those lines — the probe was broken.** The conclusion was right, as the before/after above now shows properly, but the evidence cited for it was not.\n\nAlso worth recording: `MODEL CHAIN: skipping` stays at zero even with ejection working, when *every* model on the route is benched. Ejecting all of them is deliberately bypassed — skipping a bad model is an optimisation, refusing to try anything is an outage.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **A single-model route is unaffected** (share == whole budget), but a long chain now gives each model a smaller silence budget than before. A model that legitimately takes 250s to emit its first token, on a 3-model chain with a 600s total, will now be abandoned at 200s where it previously had 600. `FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT` is the knob if that bites.\n- **Registries are keyed by ejection settings, not by provider generation.** A config reload that changes neither ejection value keeps existing benches — deliberate, since model health is still valid, and bounded by `FALLBACK_EJECT_SECONDS`.\n- The `skipped/ejected` rows added in #165 will now actually appear in the request detail.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
