    "message": "fix(routing): guarantee the chain a turn, and let a bench outlive a request\n\nTwo defects in the same subsystem, both of which made a configured fallback\nchain unreachable.\n\n1. One shared budget, drained by the first model\n------------------------------------------------\nFALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT was a single pool for the whole request, so a silent\nprimary could spend all of it and every model behind it was skipped for lack\nof time -- `MODEL CHAIN EXHAUSTED: request budget spent before trying ...`.\nMeasured on 21 days of the author's live traffic, 393 requests ran the full\n600s budget having produced only scaffolding, with a chain sitting unused.\n\nEach attempt now gets an equal share of whatever is left, counting itself and\nevery model still behind it. Time an attempt does not use flows forward, so a\nchain of fast failures leaves the last model nearly the whole budget rather\nthan a third of it.\n\nThe share bounds time-to-first-content only. Once a stream is producing, the\nattempt owns the rest of the budget: cutting a working answer to preserve a\nfallback that may not be working is the wrong trade, and PR #166 is what makes\n\"producing\" mean content rather than any frame.\n\n2. A bench that never happened\n------------------------------\n`RouteHealthRegistry` ejects a model after N consecutive failures. The comment\non its factory warned that \"a registry rebuilt per request would never reach\nits failure threshold and would eject nothing\" -- which is exactly what was\nhappening, because `MessagesHandler` and everything it owns, including the\nexecutor and its registry, is constructed per request in routes.py.\n\nRegistries now live at module scope, keyed by the two settings that define\nejection, so changing either starts a clean registry rather than inheriting\nbenches made under a different policy. An autouse fixture clears them between\ntests, since this is process state.\n\nVerification\n------------\nLive, same config, same four requests, reading the loguru log rather than\nuvicorn's:\n\n    pre-fix  (fork/main)   MODEL FALLBACK: 12   MODEL EJECTED: 0\n    post-fix (this branch) MODEL FALLBACK: 12   MODEL EJECTED: 9\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 no share, one pool again        -> silent_primary_cannot_spend_the_whole_budget\n    M2 share applies after content     -> first_token_deadline_stops_applying,\n                                          chain_is_not_extended_once_spent\n    M3 registry per-request again      -> benched_across_requests, policy_starts_clean\n    M4 one global registry regardless  -> benched_across_requests, policy_starts_clean\n\nOne existing test, `test_the_chain_is_not_extended_once_the_budget_is_spent`,\nwas rewritten rather than deleted: it pinned the old behaviour where the first\nmodel drained the pool, which is the defect. It now exercises the same\ninvariant under the new rules -- a stream past its share, on a non-streaming\nclient so the fallback stays theoretically available, proving the budget\nitself is what stops the second model being tried.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check, ty clean; pytest 4,815 passed with\nthe documented 6 test_install_ps1_* baseline failures.\n\nCorrection\n----------\nPR #165 reported \"confirmed live: four consecutive failures produced zero\nMODEL CHAIN: skipping lines\". That grep read the uvicorn log, which never\ncontains those lines -- the probe was broken. The conclusion was right, as the\nbefore/after above now shows properly, but the evidence cited for it was not.\n\nAlso note \"MODEL CHAIN: skipping\" stays at zero even with ejection working\nwhen every model on the route is benched: ejecting all of them is deliberately\nbypassed, because skipping a bad model is an optimisation and refusing to try\nanything is an outage.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
